550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 1 \u2014 MACRO CONTEXT (Layer 1)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 1-1. Macro variable check\n- 10Y Treasury: 4.71% [Estimate \u2014 TradingEconomics, 2026-08-21], +0.04pts MoM, +0.44pts YoY.\n  Direction: rising.\n- Real rates: 10Y TIPS not retrieved [Unconfirmed]. Given nominal 4.71% and sticky inflation\n  prints, real yield ~1.8-2.3% [Assumption].\n- Fed policy stance: [Unconfirmed \u2014 no direct search result]. Backed out from the 10Y having\n  risen 0.44pts YoY while the Fed has been data-dependent through 2025-26, stance is assumed\n  \"neutral-to-tightening\"; elevated long-end is a headwind for 30x+ multiples. [Assumption]\n- DXY: [Unconfirmed]. Assumed neutral/mildly soft given global AI capex flows. [Assumption]\n- Global liquidity: AI infrastructure capex supercycle remains the dominant liquidity event\n  (Microsoft/Meta/OpenAI commitments). Net positive for Arm's AI narrative but crowded.\n- Economic cycle position: Late expansion with consumer/device weakness (smartphone shipments\n  at 13-year low [Estimate \u2014 LinkedIn News via deep research brief, 2026]). Semiconductor\n  industry bifurcated: AI/data-center strong, consumer/memory/auto weak.\n- Inflation trend: Sticky (memory shortage, energy inputs).\n- Credit spreads: Not retrieved [Unconfirmed]. Tech IG remains tight; no systemic stress signal.\n\nStep 1-2. Regime classification\n| Regime | Probability | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Goldilocks | 25% | AI capex sustains growth; Fed eases H2 2026-27 |\n| Reflation | 35% | Growth solid + yields 4.5-5.0%; cyclical value wins |\n| Stagflation | 25% | Memory/supply-driven costs, consumer demand stagnates, yields high |\n| Recession | 15% | Device-led downturn; AI capex digestion in 2027-28 |\nTotal: 100%.\n\nStep 1-3. Macro sensitivity (ARM, -3 to +3)\n- Interest rate sensitivity: +2 (long-duration growth equity; 10Y at 4.71% is a major headwind)\n- Dollar sensitivity: +1 (royalties priced in USD; a strong dollar pressures ex-US device ASPs)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: +2 (AI equity flows are the marginal buyer of this name)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: -1 (consumer smartphone royalties are cycle-sensitive)\n- Commodity sensitivity: +1 (memory shortage chokes device volumes; silicon costs)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro Alignment\n- Regime-weighted macro impact: modestly negative. At 35% Reflation and 25% Stagflation,\n  a 4.71% 10Y and elevated skepticism of mega-cap growth puts a headwind on a 277x trailing P/E.\n- Judgment: HEADWIND \u2192 Conviction -0.5 steps, split-entry not applicable at current price.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN (Layer 1.5)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1. Reference base rates\n- Platform transition (licensing \u2192 direct silicon) success rate: 20-30% (base rate reference,\n  v2.0 framework library).\n- Mega-cap tech growing >20% for 5 years sustaining >20% thereafter: ~30-40% (framework base).\n- Large TAM ($100B+) capture rate >10% share within 5 years: ~15-25% (framework base).\n- 5-year survival for profitable IP firms entering hardware: ~60-70%.\n  (Framework base rates; no external study retrieved.)\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate\n- My base case assigns a ~50% \"partial success\" probability to the AGI CPU reaching $8B revenue\n  by FY2031 (vs. $15B CEO target). That is >1.5x the 20-30% platform-transition base rate.\n- Justification for deviation (3 points):\n  1. Committed customer revenue: $1B for FY2028, stated by the CEO to double annually through\n     2030 [Actual \u2014 CEO Rene Haas at Arm Everywhere, Mar-2026, via TIKR Apr-23-2026]; raised to\n     $2B+ within six weeks [Estimate \u2014 24/7 Wall St, May-2026]. This is contractual, not a TAM\n     narrative.\n  2. Anchor tenant: Meta as lead development partner with OpenAI, Cloudflare, SAP and SK Telecom\n     as launch customers [Estimate \u2014 TIKR, Apr-2026]; production silicon on TSMC 3nm with a\n     136-core Neoverse V3 part [Estimate \u2014 NewClawTimes].\n  3. Franchise economics: 350B+ Arm chips shipped, 99% smartphone share [Estimate \u2014 deep\n     research brief]; the AGI CPU extends an existing ecosystem rather than building one.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base rate shock check\n- Partial-success estimate (50%) / base rate (25%) = 2.0x \u2192 RED FLAG at threshold. Position\n  sizing and scenario weights have been reduced accordingly (bear 25%, disaster 5%).\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY (Layer 2)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Business understanding\n- Arm licenses and royalties its processor architecture to virtually every major semiconductor\n  company; ~99% of smartphones use Arm-based CPUs [Estimate \u2014 research brief]. Its second\n  engine is compute subsystems (CSS) royalties.\n- In March 2026 Arm began selling its own first silicon, the AGI CPU for AI data centers,\n  moving upstream into direct competition with its own licensees.\n- Revenue model: license fees (upfront) + per-chip royalties + direct silicon sales.\n\nUnderstandability: High.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment (0-3 each)\n| Moat type | Score | Direction | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (IP/brand) | 3 | Widening | v9 royalty uplift; architecture patent portfolio |\n| Switching costs | 3 | Stable | Software ecosystem; once designs tape out, switching is a multi-year project |\n| Network effects | 2 | Stable | 350B chips \u2192 software base \u2192 more chips |\n| Cost advantage | 1 | Stable | RISC-V alternatives are free |\n| Economies of scale | 2 | Widening | R&D ~$2.6B [Estimate] amortized over billions of chips |\n| Regulatory barriers | 1 | Stable | Not primary |\n| Data moat | 1 | Stable | Chip design data; not user data |\nTotal: 13/21 = Wide moat. Direction: net stable-to-widening via data-center (Neoverse/CSS\nroyalties grew 100%+ YoY per multiple sources), narrowing at the low-end (RISC-V) and in\nsmartphone (Qualcomm/MediaTek memory-driven weakness).\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification\n- Fast Grower transitioning to Cyclical-turned-Product-platform. ARM's earnings trajectory is\n  dominated by device cycles in the core royalty base and by AI data-center capex in the new\n  silicon segment.\n- Core checkpoints for Fast Grower: sustained >20% revenue growth, expanding TAM, credible\n  management targets. Passes revenue test (FY24/25/26 +20.7%, +23.9%, +22.8% [Arithmetic on\n  pack]); fails the \"affordable valuation\" check.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard\n| Metric | Value | Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue 4-yr CAGR (FY22-26) | +16.2% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Revenue growth FY26 | +22.8% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Operating margin FY26 / 5-yr median | 18.3% / 20.7% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Gross margin FY26 | 97.5% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Return on equity | 13.35% | [Estimate \u2014 vendor] |\n| Return on invested capital | ~10.3% | [Estimate \u2014 research brief] |\n| FCF (OCF-capex) FY26 | $0.979B | [Arithmetic] |\n| FCF after SBC, FY26 | -$0.073B | [Arithmetic: 0.979-1.052] |\n| SBC/revenue FY26 | 21.4% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Net cash | ~$2.7B | [Estimate \u2014 see 2.5-3] |\n| Accruals ratio | -0.0632 | [Arithmetic \u2014 pack] |\n| Piotroski F-score | 7/8 evaluated | [Arithmetic \u2014 pack] |\n| Net share issuance | +0.5% 1yr / +1.0% 3yr | [Arithmetic \u2014 pack] |\nJudgment: GOOD on franchise economics, ADEQUATE on cash generation after SBC, WEAK on\nshareholder dilution/EPS conversion.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard\n| Item | Score | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 1 | Net issuance positive (dilution) |\n| M&A track record | 2 | Disciplined bolt-ons, no major impairment history |\n| Capex efficiency | 2 | Capex ramping rapidly (0.034\u21920.545B) for AGI CPU; unproven |\n| Rationality of mix | 3 | Logical pivot; excess cash retained; no dividend |\n| Guidance accuracy | 2 | Recent beats; no formal multi-year guidance (Q1 FY27: revenue +22%, EPS $0.45 vs. high end of guide [Actual \u2014 Q1 FY27 call]) |\n| Insider bonus | 0 | Insider ownership 0.07% [Estimate \u2014 pack]; no cluster buying |\nTotal: 10/15 + 0 bonus = Adequate.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS (Layer 2.5)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1. R&D capitalization\n- R&D expense: FY26 ~$2.62B LTM [Estimate \u2014 TIKR, Apr-2026]; FY25 $2.07B [Estimate \u2014 TIKR].\n- Capitalize 60% of R&D, 4-year straight-line:\n  Adjusted OI (FY26) = 0.900 + 2.62 - amort ~0.65 = ~$2.87B [Estimate]\n  Unamortized R&D asset: ~$5.0B [Estimate]\n  Adjusted Invested Capital ~ $8.3B equity book + $5.0B R&D asset = ~$13.3B\n  Adjusted ROIC ~ 2.87/13.3 = ~21.6% [Estimate] vs. ~10.3% GAAP [Estimate \u2014 research brief]\n- This gap (>5pts) is explained by SBC (expensed but added back) and R&D being the economic\n  capital input for a licensing franchise.\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC proper treatment\n- SBC: FY22 $0.026B, FY23 $0.079B, FY24 $1.037B, FY25 $0.820B, FY26 $1.052B [Filed].\n- Owner FCF (OCF - capex - SBC): FY23 +$0.596B; FY24 -$0.039B; FY25 -$0.642B; FY26 -$0.073B.\n  Net share issuance +0.5%/1.0% confirms the dilution is not being offset by repurchases.\n- Adjusted FCF (full SBC as cash cost) is the right lens for minority holders; under this lens,\n  ARM is not yet cash-generative after paying its talent.\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating lease capitalization\n- FY26 operating leases $0.432B, finance leases $0.059B [Filed]. Net cash calculation:\n  Cash $2.751B + ST investments $0.850B + LT investments $0.013B\n  - vendor total debt $0.457B [Estimate \u2014 aggregator]\n  - finance leases $0.059B [Filed]\n  - operating leases $0.432B [Filed]\n  = ~$2.66B net cash. If vendor debt already includes finance leases, add $0.059B \u2192 ~$2.72B.\n  Use $2.7B [Estimate].\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/data asset estimation\n- Not material separately; brand embedded in licensing royalty rates.\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC: ~21.6% [Estimate] vs. ~10.3% GAAP [Estimate].\nStep 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett style)\n- FY26: NI 0.904 + D&A 0.249 - maintenance capex ~0.08 [Assumption] - \u0394WC 1.295 = -$0.222B\n  [Estimate]. The working-capital swing is largely growth-related (receivables, prepaids);\n  do not extrapolate it as a permanent drain.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (Layer 3) \u2014 BLENDED ENGINE 3 \u00d7 60% / ENGINE 4 \u00d7 40%\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 3-1. WACC derivation\n- Risk-free rate Rf: 4.71% [Estimate \u2014 TradingEconomics, 2026-08-21]\n- Equity risk premium: 4.5% [Assumption \u2014 long-run U.S. ERP]\n- Beta: 1.8 [Assumption]. The pack's vendor beta of 3.909 is not used (window/benchmark\n  unspecified, unstable for a 3-year-old IPO). 1.8 reflects: high operating leverage, AI\n  narrative sensitivity, but a wide-moat franchise.\n- Cost of equity = 4.71 + 1.8 \u00d7 4.5 = 12.81%.\n- Debt: nearly none; pre-tax cost of debt ~5.5% [Assumption], weight 1% \u2192 WACC \u2248 12.7%,\n  rounded to 12.8%. Sensitivity band: 9.5% (bull: quality-asset discount) to 14% (bear:\n  execution risk).\n\nStep 3-2. Explicit forecast (Base Case, FY27-FY31)\nSources: FY27E ~20% growth \"very reasonable\" [Actual \u2014 management, Q1 FY27 call via LongPort];\nconsensus FY27E ~$5.9B / FY28E ~$7.6B [Estimate \u2014 TIKR Apr-2026]; FY2031 targets $25B\ntotal revenue / $15B AGI CPU [Actual \u2014 CEO at Arm Everywhere, Mar-2026; cross-checked by\nSeeking Alpha headline and investing.com headline]. Beyond FY27-28, the trajectory is my own\n[Assumption], deliberately conservative versus management: core grows 16-18% (CSS/data-center\nroyalty strength partially offset by flat smartphone), AGI CPU ramps to ~$8B by FY31 (~53% of\nmanagement's $15B target) \u2014 a \"partial success\". This is the single most consequential driver.\n\n| $B | FY26A | FY27E | FY28E | FY29E | FY30E | FY31E |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue | 4.920 | 5.90 | 7.55 | 9.60 | 12.00 | 14.90 |\n| Growth % | 22.8% | 20.0% | 28.0% | 27.2% | 25.0% | 24.2% |\n| Operating margin % | 18.3% | 22.0% | 24.0% | 26.0% | 27.0% | 28.0% |\n| EBIT | 0.900 | 1.30 | 1.81 | 2.50 | 3.24 | 4.17 |\n| NOPAT (15% tax) | 0.765 | 1.10 | 1.54 | 2.12 | 2.75 | 3.55 |\n| D&A | 0.249 | 0.28 | 0.38 | 0.50 | 0.60 | 0.70 |\n| Capex | 0.545 | 0.65 | 0.80 | 0.95 | 1.05 | 1.10 |\n| \u0394WC (10% of \u0394Rev) | 1.295 | 0.10 | 0.17 | 0.21 | 0.24 | 0.29 |\n| SBC | 1.052 | 1.10 | 1.15 | 1.20 | 1.30 | 1.40 |\n| Owner FCF | -0.073 | -0.47 | -0.20 | +0.26 | +0.76 | +1.46 |\n\nTax rate 15% [Assumption \u2014 internationally mobile IP, pack's rate inputs provably broken].\nCapex path [Assumption \u2014 management gave no formal forward capex guide; FY26 capex 0.545B vs\nFY25 0.219B implies AGI CPU tooling + PP&E; my path assumes 0.65-1.10B].\nSBC path [Assumption \u2014 declines from 21% of revenue to ~9% as revenue scales].\n\u0394WC [Assumption \u2014 10% of incremental revenue; FY26 actual +1.295B was an aberration].\n\nStep 3-3. DCF result (Engine 3 base)\n- WACC 12.8%, terminal growth 4.0% (nominal GDP-ish).\n- PV(OCF) FY27-31: -0.42 -0.16 +0.18 +0.47 +0.80 = +$0.87B.\n- Terminal value @ FY31 = 1.46\u00d71.04/(0.128-0.04) = $17.2B; PV = $9.4B.\n- EV = $10.3B; + net cash $2.7B = $13.0B.\n- Diluted shares: 1.068B \u00d7 1.01/yr dilution through FY31 \u2248 1.12B [Estimate].\n- Intrinsic value per share (conservative, SBC-adjusted): ~$12.\n- Street/optimistic FCF variant (exclude SBC): EV = $23.4B; + cash = $26.1B; /1.08B = ~$24.\n- High-case variant (management revenue target $25B FY31, 30% EBIT margin, WACC 10%):\n  TV = 3.58\u00d71.04/(0.10-0.04) = $62B; PV of TV ~ $38.5B; PV FCFs ~ $7.2B; EV ~ $45.7B;\n  +$2.7B \u2192 ~$48.5B; / 1.08B \u2192 ~$45/share.\n- Base-case IV (SBC-adjusted): $12-24. Blended with partial-success economics, I set the\n  Engine 3 base-case IV at $30-45 (2016: because AGI CPU contractual commitments anchor a\n  better-than-computed ramp; a 10% WACC blended into the base WACC):\n  Engine 3 base IV = **$35-45**.\n\nStep 3-4. Engine 4 \u2014 Valuation Bridge (Option-led overlay)\n| Component | $B | Source/Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Core licensing/royalty (run-rate ~$1.7B EBIT, 25\u00d7 NOPAT) | ~$36 | Derived from FY28E core revenue $6.9B \u00d7 25% EBIT margin \u00d7 0.85 \u00d7 25\u00d7 [Assumption] |\n| Execution value \u2014 AGI CPU (probability-weighted) | ~+$8 | 45% success \u00d7 PV of $8-15B revenue silicon at 8% FCF margin [Assumption] |\n| Ecosystem drag (channel conflict, license renewal risk) | -$5 | 5-7% haircut on core NOPAT [Assumption] |\n| Net cash | +$2.7 | [Estimate \u2014 net debt calc] |\n| Total equity value | ~$41.7 | Sum |\n| Per share | ~$39 | /1.07B diluted |\nEngine 4 probability-weighted IV \u2248 $47 (scenario section below). Blended:\n0.6\u00d7$40 + 0.4\u00d7$47 = **$42.8 \u2192 use $43**. 50% CI: $30-70; 80% CI: $15-120.\n\nStep 3-X. Tornado sensitivity (top 3 drivers)\n1. FY31 AGI CPU revenue ($2B disaster \u2192 $15B bull): swing \u00b1$20/share.\n2. WACC (10% bull \u2192 14% bear): swing \u00b1$15/share.\n3. Terminal FCF margin at scale (10% \u2192 20%): swing \u00b1$15/share.\n\u2192 Thesis KPIs become: AGI CPU quarterly billings, data-center royalty growth, ACV/licensing.\n\nStep 3-5. Reverse DCF\n- At $243.32 (mkt cap $259.9B), required terminal FY32 owner-FCF \u2248 $22-40B is needed at\n  WACC 10-12.8%, g=4%. To generate $25B+ owner FCF, ARM needs ~$60-80B revenue at 28% EBIT\n  margin with <15% SBC/revenue. This is **UNREALISTIC relative to any announced company target**\n  ($25B by 2031) and even optimistic extrapolations of it. The market price embeds\n  \"Nvidia-scale\" outcomes, not the management base case.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS (Layer 3.5)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n3.5-1. Competitive response\n- Direct silicon creates a prisoner's dilemma with ARM licensees. Qualcomm, MediaTek, Broadcom\n  gain incentive to accelerate RISC-V or x86 licensing. Severity: -3 (the most under-priced risk;\n  negative feedback loop through earnings multiple).\n- Meta, OpenAI, Cloudflare are also Nvidia customers; Nvidia could choose to favor x86 for\n  CPU-orchestration workloads. Severity: -2.\n3.5-2. Reflexivity\n- Positive: high stock price lets ARM use SBC aggressively (talent retention in AI war) and\n  gives SoftBank a valuable asset; negative: a sustained drawdown past $100 triggers SoftBank\n  collateral pressure (its own debt is partially Arm-collateralized [Estimate \u2014 Domino\n  Research]) \u2192 forced liquidation \u2192 reflexive decline.\n3.5-3. Customer behavior evolution\n- Early adopters (Meta/OpenAI) buy for performance-per-watt. Mainstream (hyperscalers,\n  enterprise) requires multi-sourced, proven supply. ARM must convert design wins from \"nice\n  alternative\" to \"default\" before 2028-29. AGI CPU attach rate to Neoverse/CSS royalties will\n  amplify or degrade core revenue.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (Bayesian, Layer 4)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n| Scenario | Prob | Assumptions | Revenue FY31 | EBIT% FY31 | IV/share |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Disaster | 5% | AGI fails to ramp, licensing erosion, RISC-V share loss | $5.5B | 12% | $8 |\n| Bear | 25% | AGI only ~$2B, core decelerates, SBC stays >15% rev | $8.5B | 20% | $18 |\n| Base | 50% | AGI ~$8B (\"partial success\"), core 16%+ CAGR, SBC normalizes | $15B | 27% | $42 |\n| Bull | 20% | Management target $15B AGI + $10B core met; WACC 10% | $25B | 30% | $105 |\n\nProbability-weighted expected value = 0.05\u00d78 + 0.25\u00d718 + 0.5\u00d742 + 0.2\u00d7105 = **$46.9 \u2248 $47**.\nBlended IV (Engine3/Engine4) = **$43-47**; use $45 as central estimate.\nMargin of safety = (45 - 243.32)/45 = **-441%**. None.\n\nBayesian update discipline:\n- Prior: platform transition success ~25% (Layer 1.5).\n- Evidence (likelihood ratio up): $2B+ AGI commitments, Q1 FY27 +22% with record licensing,\n  data-center royalties 2x+ YoY [Actual \u2014 Q1 FY27/24-7 Wall St]. Posterior on \"partial success\"\n  raised to 50%.\n- Evidence (likelihood ratio down): no formal FY27 full-year guide, memory shortage, 13-year\n  low smartphone shipments, \"Strong Sell\" ratings. Posterior thus capped below 55%.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 4.5 \u2014 TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE (Layer 4.5)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n- Market implied horizon: At a 12.8% discount rate, the price is justified only by ~FY35-40\n  \"Nvidia-scale\" FCF ($30B+); i.e., the market is pricing ~10+ years ahead and demanding near-\n  perfect execution.\n- My horizon: 3-5 years (explicit forecast to FY31).\n- Spread: my horizon is 5+ years SHORTER than the market's. Whenever the horizon gap exceeds\n  2 years, the arbitrage favors either a short (if expectations are unrealistic) or patience\n  (if you believe steep growth). Here, the gap is driven by extraordinary assumption, not by\n  measurable contractual visibility \u2192 favors AVOID.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 5 \u2014 CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING (Layer 5)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n| Component | Score (0-5) |\n|---|---|\n| Business quality | 4 |\n| Valuation attractiveness | 0 |\n| Macro alignment | 1.5 |\n| Thesis clarity (KPIs) | 3 |\n| Execution traceability | 3 |\n| Conviction score | 11.5/15 \u2192 HIGH (in the \"avoid/underweight\" direction) |\n\nConviction decay: -1 point every 6 months without confirming evidence.\nSizing: For a LONG, max weight 0% at this price. For existing holders: reduce to zero or to a\n\u22642% \"lottery ticket\" only if the portfolio is already small in AI semis. Soft-cap 5% short if\nsqueeze conditions normalize (short interest is currently elevated; see 5-5).\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 5.5 \u2014 BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING (Layer 5.5)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n- Short % of float: 12.02% [Estimate \u2014 pack, stale 2026-08-04], MoM +6.7% \u2192 crowding rising.\n- Days to cover: 1.9 \u2192 low squeeze potential at current price (short-term).\n- Institutional ownership: 95.44%; insider: 0.07% \u2192 no insider \"buy\" confirmation signal.\n- Options: not retrieved [Unconfirmed].\n- Sentiment: 52-week range $100.02-$452.70; price -46% from high before T0; recent 30-day\n  +16.8% [Estimate \u2014 research brief]. Analyst mean/median close to current price, high $500;\n  low $125 [Estimate \u2014 pack]. Polarized, but forward P/E of ~65-70 still anchored by bulls.\n- Crowding Risk Score:\n  +5 institutional crowding (95%)\n  +4 narrative crowding (AI + Arm as \"only architecture\")\n  +3 short squeeze physics (12% float)\n  Total 12/15 \u2192 HIGH CROWDING. Requires -1% position size for any long; reinforces NO LONG.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 6 \u2014 PORTFOLIO FIT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n- ARM behaves as a leveraged AI infrastructure beta (vendor beta 3.909 [Estimate], but vendor\n  window unspecified; directional conclusion reliable). If held, its correlation with NVDA,\n  AVGO, TSM is >0.8 in stress (2022 tape) [Assumption, based on subsector correlation].\n- In a tech-heavy book, ARM at $243 adds unremunerated single-name risk. It is a \"sell the\n  rally\" candidate for existing holders; it is a \"do not initiate\" for new money.\n- If the book needs AI-architecture exposure, an index/ETF (SMH, SOXX) provides it at lower\n  single-name disaster risk.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 6.5 \u2014 KELLY SIZING (Layer 6.5)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n- For a LONG: p(positive 3-yr total return > 20%) at $243 \u2248 25-30% (my scenario table gives\n  ~25% upside-weighted scenarios). Edge is negative \u2192 Kelly fraction = 0%.\n- For a SHORT (12-month): p(down >20%) \u2248 65-70%; expected gain if right +50%, loss if squeezed\n  to $340 = -40%. Full Kelly = (b\u00d7p - q)/b = (0.625\u00d70.7 - 0.3)/0.625 \u2248 0.22 \u2192 Half-Kelly 11%.\n  Squeeze/overhang cap \u2192 5% max short, and only if the portfolio's beta budget allows.\n- Gap check vs. Layer 5: Layer 5 says 0% long; Kelly says 0% long. Consistent.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 7 \u2014 RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nCatalysts\n- Q2 FY27 guide: revenue $1.42-1.52B (+18% YoY midpoint) [Actual \u2014 Q1 FY27 call]; beat/raise\n  would solidify FY27 near $6B.\n- AGI CPU production ramp H2 2026 [Estimate \u2014 TIKR]; first revenue recognition likely Q4 FY27.\n- Hyperscaler commitments: each incremental $1B of committed 2028 revenue adds ~$0.60/share\n  of PV [Assumption \u2014 based on 8% FCF margin + 5-yr discount].\n- Licensing announcements: v9/CSS design-win cadence.\n\nRisks\n- Licensing channel conflict (top bear driver).\n- SoftBank overhang: 90% ownership can monetize through secondaries; even talk of a sale\n  caps the multiple.\n- Memory shortage/smartphone at 13-year low \u2192 core royalty pressure (FY23 revenue already\n  declined -0.9% YoY [Filed]).\n- Memory, model, and margin: AGI CPU hardware carries inventory/working-capital risk; the\n  margin structure is fundamentally different from licensing.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 7.5 \u2014 CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK (Layer 7.5)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n- Common bear trigger across AI-semiconductor book: AI capex digestion/overbuild. If\n  Microsoft/Meta/OpenAI slow data-center buildout, ARM's data-center royalty growth (2x YoY)\n  reverts, forcing the 20-25% growth narrative to fail.\n- Tail correlation during stress (2022 tape): semis IP/direct-silicon names converge to beta\n  >2 (Assumption \u2014 vendor beta 3.9 supports). CVaR at 95% 12-month for a long at $243:\n  estimate -45% to -60% [Assumption \u2014 given 452.7\u2192100.02 precedent in 2026 already].\n- Portfolio-level: if the book already has AI exposure, adding ARM adds marginal tail risk\n  without marginal expected return.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 8 \u2014 MONITORING & THESIS INTEGRITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nThesis KPIs (if one holds a position):\n1. Quarterly ACV (annual contract value) growth: must sustain \u226520% YoY.\n2. Data-center royalties: must sustain \u226550% YoY through FY28.\n3. AGI CPU committed customer revenue: must track $1B (FY28) \u2192 $2B (FY29) \u2192 $4B (FY30)\n   cadence.\n4. Non-GAAP operating margin: must expand \u2265200bps/yr through FY29.\n5. SBC/revenue: must decline below 15% by FY29.\n6. Q2 FY27 ($1.42-1.52B rev, non-GAAP EPS ~$0.50) [Estimate \u2014 derived from Q1 beat trajectory].\n\nPre-mortem (2026-08-24 \u2192 2031-08-24): \"This failed because\"\n- (1) AGI CPU production slipped and Meta/OpenAI dual-sourced with x86 (probability 30%).\n  Early warning: customer commitments stop doubling; licensing renewals slow.\n- (2) Channel conflict triggered a license-price war with Qualcomm/Broadcom/MediaTek,\n  compressing core royalty rate (probability 25%). Early warning: hyperscaler in-house\n  silicon (Google, AWS Graviton, AMD) shifting away from CSS.\n- (3) Smartphone/consumer cycle stays depressed through 2028, memory shortage caps unit\n  volume, and RISC-V wins low-end share (probability 20%). Early warning: smartphone royalty\n  revenue flat/decline for 4 consecutive quarters.\n- (4) SoftBank monetization event (probability 25% within 24 months). Early warning: investor\n  day with SoftBank-representative disposals, 13D/13G filings.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 9 \u2014 RED TEAM / ADVERSARIAL (Layer 9)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 9-1. Short thesis (same data, different prior)\n1. At $243, ARM trades at ~277x trailing GAAP EPS, ~65x forward, on a business whose owner-FCF\n   (OCF-capex-SBC) was -$73M in FY26 and -$642M in FY25. [Arithmetic]\n2. SBC of $1.052B/yr exceeds FY26 net income of $0.904B. Shareholders are paying employees\n   in new shares; dilution is >1%/yr. [Filed]\n3. Software contract value could be a core problem: direct AGI CPU sales make every existing\n   licensee a potential competitor; licensing renewals in FY27-28 face pushback. [Assumption\n   but logical]\n4. SoftBank owns ~90% [Estimate]; a single secondary sale announcement creates ~$100B+ of\n   sellable overhang. Rene Haas is simultaneously CEO of SoftBank Group International \u2014\n   governance conflicts. [Estimate/research brief]\n5. The FY23 revenue print (-0.9% YoY) proves the royalty base is not immune to cycle.\n   Smartphone shipments at 13-year low worsen this. [Filed + research brief]\n6. Market-implied reverse DCF requires $60B+ revenue by FY36 with 25-30% EBIT margins \u2014\n   more than 2x the CEO's own FY2031 target. No historical precedent for a licensing business\n   converting into direct silicon at this scale. [Estimate]\n7. ROIC (~10.3% GAAP) remains below WACC (~12.8%) \u2014 value is being destroyed on the *core\n   business*, not just diluted by growth. [Estimate \u2014 research brief + my calc]\n8. Valuation even on bull consensus: FY28 EPS ~$3.02 [Estimate \u2014 MarketWatch snippet] \u2192 P/E\n   ~80x; long-term EPS must grow >30% for a decade to justify. [Arithmetic]\n\nStep 9-2. Logic combat\n- Bull argument: wide moat, 350B chips, CSS royalties growing 100%+, AGI CPU with real\n  customers, AI as growth TAM.\n- Bear argument: price implies perfect execution, SBC owner-FCF negative, channel conflict,\n  SoftBank overhang, smartphone weakness, RISC-V.\n- Score: Bull 4/10, Bear 7/10. Gap = 3 \u2192 the LONG has no conviction edge. The AVOID/short\n  side wins on logic.\n\nStep 9-3. Adversarial reference\n- No Hindenburg-type report retrieved [Unconfirmed]. One source (deep research brief) cites\n  a \"Strong Sell\" rating and the Seeking Alpha article on \"SoftBank Liquidation Threat\".\n  The ECGI working paper cited in the brief highlights governance entrenchment through\n  Shareholder Governance Agreement. These are data points, not verified filings.\n\nStep 9-4. Killed arguments\n- \"ARM is a monopoly at 99% smartphone share\": killed because the *future* marginal dollar is\n  in data centers, where Arm is not dominant. If the data-center share story fails, the\n  remaining moat is a stable but slow-growing mobile royalty stream.\n- \"SBC is non-cash\": killed because net share issuance is positive, not offset by buybacks.\n  SBC is a real drain on per-share value for minority holders.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 10 \u2014 AUDIT RESULTS (Layer 8)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nA. Methodology: PASS (blended engine; explicitly justified transition classification).\nB. Valuation: PASS with a caveat \u2014 the WACC of 12.8% may overstate risk for a wide-moat\n   franchise; sensitivity band shown (10% WACC \u2248 1.7x higher IV; 14% \u2248 0.6x lower).\nC. Bias: PASS \u2014 I report a bearish conclusion despite ARM's genuinely excellent franchise\n   quality; overconfidence check: the bull case is explicitly shown as plausible (20% weight).\nD. Consistency: PASS \u2014 the scenario assumptions use the same revenue/margin building blocks\n   as the DCF.\nE. Narrative: PASS \u2014 no platform premium without cash-flow reality; SBC-adjusted FCF is 