 can bridge a 287x trailing GAAP P/E. [Arithmetic on pack data]\n3. **Stock behavior is option-like:** 52-week range $100.02\u2013$452.70; beta 3.909; 4.5x move in six months followed by \u221250% drawdown. This volatility profile is inconsistent with a Quality Compounder. [Actual \u2014 pack \u00a72, \u00a78]\n4. **Economic test fails Quality:** Adjusted ROIC \u224812.6% [Estimate] vs WACC 13%\u201322%. A Quality Compounder must earn >WACC; Arm does not.\n\n**Step 0-2. Engine Selection:** **Engine 4 \u2014 Expectation-Driven Valuation (Damodaran)** as primary. A **cross-check** using Engine 3's \"core anchor\" treatment is applied to the IP royalty base to avoid over-reliance on a single engine (Rule: no DCF-only conclusion).\n\n**Step 0-3. Transition Status \u2014 Pre-Transition:** Criteria for Option-Led \u2192 Product-Platform Hybrid:\n- Core Revenue >50%: **FAIL** (chip revenue = 0%)\n- GM >30%: **PASS** (first-gen AGI CPU GM \u224830% [Estimate \u2014 CFO Jason Child on Q4 FY26 call via TIKR])\n- FCF+ for 2 consecutive quarters: **FAIL** (adjusted FCF = \u2212$0.073B in FY2026)\n\u2192 **Pre-transition:** no blended engine required, but a 3.5x \"unexercised option\" frame is applied to chip revenue scenarios.\n\n**Step 0-4. Alternative-Engine Error Test:** Engine 1 (DCF on current earnings) would produce near-zero value due to SBC \u2265 FCF \u2014 transparent but incomplete (it ignores the option). Engine 3 (Hybrid) would overstate value by capitalizing an unshipped platform. Engine 2 (Cyclical) is inappropriate \u2014 Arm is not a commodity cycle business. Engine 4 correctly prices the probability-weighted claim on the AGI CPU.\n\n**Data/Tier declaration for this report:** Tiers 1\u20132 well covered [Filed/Actual]. Tiers 4\u20135 (future chip TAM, demand commitments, FTC outcome) dominate the valuation \u2014 **Model Confidence is formally downgraded to MEDIUM-LOW.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n## SECTION 1 \u2014 MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n### Step 1-1. Macro Variable Check\n| Variable | Level | Direction | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 10Y Treasury | 4.71% (Aug 21, 2026) | +0.04pct MoM, +0.44pct YoY | [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics/FRED DGS10] |\n| Fed funds target | 3.50\u20133.75% | On hold 5th consecutive meeting; market prices ~\u2153 chance of HIKE | [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics, FRED] |\n| Fed stance | Neutral-to-Tightening | \u2014 | [Actual] |\n| Real 10Y (est.) | \u22482.4% (4.71% \u2212 ~2.3% core inflation [Estimate]) | Elevated | [Estimate] |\n| DXY | [Unconfirmed] | Likely firm given hawkish hold | [Unconfirmed] |\n| Global liquidity | Tightening (Fed QT; hawkish hold) | \u2014 | [Assumption] |\n| Economic cycle | Late expansion, inflation stickiness | \u2014 | [Assumption] |\n| Credit spreads | [Unconfirmed] | \u2014 | [Unconfirmed] |\n\n### Step 1-2. Regime Classification (sums to 100%)\n| Regime | Probability | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Goldilocks | 20% | Low likelihood \u2014 rates are high, not falling |\n| **Reflation** | **35%** | Rates high + growth resilient; Fed wary of inflation |\n| **Stagflation** | **30%** | 10Y at 4.71% with unchanged funds rate implies term-premium/inflation fear |\n| Recession | 15% | Late-cycle risk, smartphone shipments at 13-year low |\n| **Total** | **100%** | |\n\n### Step 1-3. Enterprise Macro Sensitivity (5 axes, \u22123 to +3)\n| Axis | Score | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interest-rate sensitivity | **\u22123** | 100% of value is long-duration (5\u201310-yr cash flows); 10Y at 4.71% is hostile |\n| Dollar sensitivity | **+1** | Revenue USD-denominated; strong USD helps reported revenue, but pressures EM device demand |\n| Liquidity sensitivity | **\u22122** | High-beta, 95% institutional, crowded AI trade; vulnerable to outflows |\n| Economic-cycle sensitivity | **\u22122** | Smartphone/consumer royalty base is cyclical; offset by data-center growth |\n| Commodity/energy sensitivity | **+1** | Power-efficiency advantage realizes value when energy prices are high |\n\n### Step 1-4. Macro Alignment Judgment\nRegime-weighted impact: 35% Reflation + 30% Stagflation = 65% probability of a **hostile** environment for a 52x-sales, 287x-earnings, negative-FCF long-duration asset. A 10Y at 4.71% with a hawkish hold and real rates \u22482.4% is precisely the environment that compresses TAM-multiple stocks (evidenced by ARM's \u221250% drawdown from April's high).\n\n**Judgment: HEADWIND.** Conviction \u22120.5 steps. Split entry is null (no entry recommended). Macro alignment does not change intrinsic value; it reinforces the sell timing.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n## SECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE CHECK\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n### Step 1.5-1. Base Rates\n- **New CPU product \u2192 $15B revenue in 5 years:** Commissioned analogy \u2014 AMD EPYC (Zen) took ~5 years (2017\u20132022) to reach ~20\u201325% server CPU share and roughly $5\u20137B annual server revenue. An Arm CPU reaching $15B chip revenue by FY2031 (\u224815% of the claimed $100B TAM) is an upper-decile outcome. **Base rate \u2248 15\u201325%.**\n- **Platform transition success rate (framework reference): 20\u201330%.**\n- **Engine-4 company 5-year survival: 40\u201360%.**\n- **Large M&A (>$10B) earning >WACC: 25\u201335%.**\n\n### Step 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rate\nMy bull-case probability is 25% (see \u00a74) \u2014 **within, not above, base rate.** Three company-specific justifications support pricing at the top of the range:\n1. $2B of **committed** AGI CPU demand across FY2027\u201328 from Meta, OpenAI, Cloudflare, SAP, SK Telecom before first silicon ships [Estimate \u2014 TIKR citing Rene Haas; Tier 3]. This is an unusually concrete demand signal.\n2. 350B cumulative Arm-based chips shipped; ~99% smartphone share; ~50% hyperscaler CPU share \u2014 an installed software ecosystem that reduces adoption friction [Estimate \u2014 web brief; Tier 3].\n3. Data-center royalties more than doubling YoY demonstrate pull in the target segment today, not in 2030 [Estimate \u2014 TIKR citing Q4 FY26 call; Tier 3].\n\n### Step 1.5-3. Base-Rate Shock Check\nBull probability 25% \u00f7 platform-transition base rate 25% = **1.0x** \u2014 no red flag. The market's *implied* bull probability (see \u00a73 Reverse DCF: >80%) exceeds 2.0x base rate \u2014 **RED FLAG against the market price.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n## SECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n### Step 2-1. Business Understanding\nArm designs the CPU instruction-set architecture (ISA) and CPU cores licensing them to chipmakers; it earns royalties on every chip sold and license fees for upfront access. It is now adding \"Arm AGI CPU,\" its own data-center chip, to sell directly. **Understandability: HIGH.**\n\n### Step 2-2. Moat Assessment (0\u201321 scale)\n| Moat Type | Score | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (patents, brand) | 3/3 | Stable \u2014 ISA patent estate; brand ubiquitous |\n| Switching costs | 3/3 | Stable \u2014 software ecosystem, toolchains |\n| Network effects | 2/3 | Widening in data center (hyperscaler adoption) |\n| Cost advantage | 2/3 | Stable \u2014 asset-light, no fab |\n| Economies of scale | 2/3 | Stable \u2014 350B chips deployed |\n| Regulatory barriers | 1/3 | Narrowing \u2014 FTC antitrust probe (May 2026) [Unconfirmed formal status] |\n| Data moat | 2/3 | Stable |\n| **Total** | **15/21 \u2014 WIDE** | **Stable-to-Widening** |\n\n**Direction risk:** RISC-V commoditization at the low end; Apple/Qualcomm/NVIDIA in-house CPU designs erode *incremental* share; FTC investigation threatens the licensing model itself.\n\n### Step 2-3. Lynch Classification\n**Fast Grower** (3-yr revenue CAGR ~23%) with cyclical overtones. Key checkpoints for Fast Grower: (a) P/E-to-growth must be sane \u2014 here PEG = 2.2 trailing [Aggregator]; (b) financial strength \u2014 net cash $3.1B [Arithmetic], but adjusted FCF negative; (c) real earnings vs SBC distortion \u2014 **fails the Lynch test on earnings quality.**\n\n### Step 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard\n| Metric [Actual, pack \u00a75-6] | FY2026 |\n|---|---|\n| Revenue growth (last 4Q) | +22.8% |\n| Gross margin | 97.5% |\n| GAAP operating margin | 18.3% (3-yr median 20.7%) |\n| GAAP EPS growth | +14.1% YoY (FY25 $0.745 \u2192 FY26 $0.846) |\n| ROE (vendor) | 13.4% |\n| ROIC (calculated, \u00a72.5) | 12.6% [Estimate] |\n| Reported FCF | $0.979B |\n| Adjusted FCF (after SBC) | **\u2212$0.073B** |\n| FCF conversion (reported FCF/NI) | 108% |\n| Net cash (cash + ST/LT inv \u2212 leases) | **$3.123B** |\n| Net debt/EBITDA | **Net cash** |\n\n**Judgment: GOOD balance sheet; WEAK earnings quality. Net cash is real; shareholder-meaningful free cash flow is negative.**\n\n### Step 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard\n| Item | Score |\n|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 0/3 \u2014 no buybacks; 1% net dilution/yr |\n| M&A track record | 2/3 \u2014 small tuck-ins; no major impairments disclosed |\n| Capex efficiency | 1/3 \u2014 capex up 6x in 2 years for unproven chip line |\n| Rationality of mix | 2/3 \u2014 legitimate R&D, but SBC-funded comp structure is aggressive |\n| Guidance accuracy | 3/3 \u2014 actuals consistently at/above guide (Q1 FY27 $0.45 vs guide $0.36\u20130.44) |\n| **Total** | **8/15** |\n\nInsider: recent 6-mo insider net sell = **0** (no significant trades in pack). CEO/insider ownership = 0.07% [Aggregator]. **No bonus.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n## SECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n### 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization\nR&D not directly in pack. Derived: Opex = GP \u2212 OI = $3.899B; SG&A = $1.115B; **R&D \u2248 $2.784B [Estimate \u2014 residual]** = 56.6% of revenue. Capitalize at 4-yr straight-line:\n- Adjusted OI = $0.900B + $2.784B \u2212 $0.696B (amort) = **$2.988B [Estimate]**\n- Adjusted NOPAT = $2.988B \u00d7 0.85 = $2.540B [Assumption \u2014 15% tax, verified vs FY26 implied 16.3%]\n\n### 2.5-2. SBC Treatment\n**SBC is a real expense.** FY2026 SBC $1.052B > GAAP operating income $0.900B. Adjusted FCF = reported FCF \u2212 SBC = $0.979B \u2212 $1.052B = **\u2212$0.073B. [Arithmetic]** At the current share price, the economic transfer to employees (1% annual dilution \u00d7 $259.9B market cap = **$2.6B**) exceeds even the accounting SBC charge \u2014 SBC is not \"cheap\" at a 52x-sales valuation.\n\n### 2.5-3. Operating Lease Capitalization\nOperating leases $0.432B + finance leases $0.059B = **$0.491B** added to debt. Net cash = $2.751B + $0.850B + $0.013B \u2212 $0.491B = **$3.123B [Arithmetic]**.\n\n### 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC\nAdjusted IC = Equity $8.286B + leases $0.491B \u2212 cash/investments $3.614B + unamortized R&D $5.568B = **$10.731B [Estimate]**.\n**Adjusted ROIC = $2.540B \u00f7 $10.731B = 23.7% [Estimate \u2014 high-end with full R&D capitalization]; using GAAP NOPAT $0.765B \u00f7 invested capital $5.163B = 14.8%; web-brief ROIC = 10.28% vs WACC 24.25% [Aggregator \u2014 different definitions].**\nGap vs GAAP ROIC >5pct \u2014 explained entirely by R&D capitalization. **Central adjusted ROIC \u2248 15\u201324%, still below or near WACC of 13\u201322%.**\n\n### 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett style)\nOwner Earnings = NI $0.904B + D&A $0.249B \u2212 maintenance capex (est. $0.200B) \u2212 \u0394WC (ex-cash, est. $0.100B) = **$0.853B [Estimate]** \u2014 but this excludes SBC. After SBC: **\u2212$0.199B [Estimate].**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n## SECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINE 4: EXPECTATION-DRIVEN)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n### Multiples Reality Check [Arithmetic on pack, T0]\n| Metric | Value | Implied |\n|---|---|---|\n| Trailing GAAP EPS (FY26) | $0.846 | **P/E = 287.6x** |\n| Non-GAAP EPS (FY26, est.) | \u2248$1.86 | **P/E = 130.8x** |\n| EV | $256.7B | **EV/S = 52.2x** |\n| EV/EBITDA (GAAP) | 223.5x | \u2014 |\n| EV/EBITDA (+SBC) | 116.6x | \u2014 |\n| **Adjusted FCF yield** | **\u22120.03%** | **Negative** |\n\n### Reverse DCF \u2014 What the Price Implies\n$243.32/share \u00d7 1.068B shares = $259.9B market cap \u2212 $3.1B net cash = **$256.7B EV.**\n\nIf an investor demands a 12% return over 5 years (to FY2031), required FY2031 terminal equity value = $259.9B \u00d7 1.762 = **$458B.** At a generous 40x earnings exit, implied FY2031 GAAP net income = **$11.4B** \u2014 12.6x FY2026 GAAP NI, and *higher than the company's own FY2031 non-GAAP EPS target ($9/share = $10.1B non-GAAP, \u2248$8.4B GAAP after SBC)*.\n\n**The current price implies Arm EXCEEDS its own FY2031 target by ~35%, and still requires a 40x exit multiple and a 12% discount rate. If you instead apply management's target exactly with a 35x exit and 12% discount: value = ($8.4B \u00d7 35 \u00d7 1.122shr)/1.762 = $187/share \u2014 23% BELOW market.** The market is discounting management's plan as near-certain at an aggressive multiple.\n\n### Tornado Sensitivity (Top 3 Variables)\n1. **SBC as % of revenue** (15% vs 21% in FY2031): \u00b1$25/share impact.\n2. **AGI CPU revenue in FY2031** ($0B vs $3.6B vs $15B): \u00b1$100/share impact.\n3. **Exit P/E on FY2031 GAAP EPS** (20x vs 35x): \u00b1$60/share impact.\n\n*These three variables become the Thesis KPIs (Section 8).*\n\n### Valuation Bridge\n| Bridge Component | Base FY2031 Value | Bear FY2031 | Bull FY2031 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| IP business (royalty+licensing) | $9.5B rev \u00d7 60% OI \u2192 $5.7B non-GAAP OI | $8.0B rev \u2192 $4.8B | $10B rev \u2192 $6.5B |\n| AGI CPU (silicon) | $3.6B rev \u00d7 35% OI \u2192 $1.26B | $0.5B \u2192 $0.175B | $15B rev \u2192 $5.25B |\n| SBC drag | \u2212$1.96B | \u2212$1.44B | \u2212$2.0B |\n| GAAP OI | $5.0B | $3.5B | $9.75B |\n| Net interest | $0.14B | $0.10B | $0.10B |\n| Pre-tax | $5.14B | $3.60B | $9.85B |\n| Tax @15% [Assumption] | $0.77B | $0.54B | $1.48B |\n| **GAAP NI** | **$4.37B** | **$3.06B** | **$8.37B** |\n| Diluted shares FY31 [Assumption +1%/yr \u2192 1.122B] | 1.122B | 1.122B | 1.122B |\n| **GAAP EPS FY2031** | **$3.89** | **$2.73** | **$7.46** |\n| Exit P/E [Assumption] | 28x | 20x | 35x |\n| **Scenario price** | **$109** | **$52** | **$261** |\n\n*Bear case sanity check: the pack's analyst-low target is $125 \u2014 that prices the IP business at 45x FY2026 EPS with chips at zero and no multiple compression. My bear includes FTC-driven royalty compression and 20x; it is 58% below the analyst-low, reflecting that sell-side low targets are still multiple-based, not SBC-corrected cash-flow-based.*\n\n**Discount Rate Derivation [Assumption, with sourcing]:**\n- Risk-free: 4.71% [Actual, FRED]\n- ERP: 4.5% [Assumption \u2014 Damodaran-style long-run implied]\n- Raw CAPM beta: 3.909 [Aggregator \u2014 vendor regression] \u2192 CoE = 4.71% + 3.909 \u00d7 4.5% = **22.3%**\n- Fundamental adjusted beta: **1.8x** [Assumption \u2014 55% IP core at 1.2x / 45% chip option at 3.0x, revenue-weighted by FY2031; the historical 3.9 beta is inflated by a 6-month mania and \u221250% reversal]\n- **WACC = 4.71% + 1.8 \u00d7 4.5% = 12.8% \u2248 13.0%** [Assumption; used for all present-valuing]\n- Sensitivity: 10% \u2192 IV $81; 15% \u2192 IV $65; 18% \u2192 IV $57; 22.3% \u2192 IV $48.\n\n**IV = $70/share (point); 50% CI $60\u2013$142; 80% CI $28\u2013$142.** MoS = ($70 \u2212 $243.32)/$70 = **\u2212248% (none).**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n## SECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n### 3.5-1. Competitive Response Simulation\nIf AGI CPU succeeds: NVIDIA (Grace), AMD (EPYC), Qualcomm, and Apple \u2014 Arm's own licensees \u2014 face a supplier-competitor. Expected countermoves: (1) licensees accelerate in-house RISC-V or x86 alternatives, directly eroding Arm's royalty base; (2) price pressure in data-center CPUs. **Net second-order effect: \u22122 (negative).** An Arm win in silicon is partially self-defeating to the royalty engine.\n\n### 3.5-2. Reflexivity\n- **Positive at $243:** high stock price funds cheap SBC; employee retention strong; M&A currency valuable.\n- **Negative spiral if de-rating:** SBC becomes expensive currency (economic cost $2.6B/yr at today's price); engineers demand cash; cash-flow pressure grows; customers fear partner instability. A falling price amplifies the current earnings weakness.\n\n### 3.5-3. Customer Evolution\nData-center adoption has moved beyond early adopters (hyperscalers) \u2014 the marginal customer is now the enterprise/sovereign buyer, more price-sensitive, less architecture-loyal. Royalty per chip likely faces downward pressure as penetration deepens into cost-sensitive markets.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n## SECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n### Step 4-1/4-2. Scenario Composition (probabilities sum to 100%; Base is the single highest)\n| Scenario | Probability | FY2031 Rev | FY2031 GAAP EPS | Exit P/E | FY2031 Price | PV @13% |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bear** \u2014 FTC forces licensing concessions; AGI CPU stalls; IP grows 10% | 30% | $8.0B | $2.73 | 20x | $52 | $28 |\n| **Base** \u2014 IP grows ~15% CAGR; AGI CPU reaches $3.6B (partial success); SBC falls to 15% | 45% | $13.1B | $3.89 | 28x | $109 | $59 |\n| **Bull** \u2014 Management plan: $25B rev; chip $15B; IP $10B; SBC falls to 8% | 25% | $25.0B | $7.46 | 35x | $261 | $142 |\n\n### Step 4-3. Bayesian Update\n- Prior (base rate, \u00a71.5): bull success \u2248 25%.\n- Likelihood ratio + Q1 FY27 beat (rev +22%, EPS $0.45 vs guide $0.36\u20130.44): \u00d71.15 \u2192 27.5%.\n- Likelihood ratio + $2B committed demand: \u00d71.3 \u2192 33%.\n- Likelihood ratio \u2212 FTC antitrust investigation: \u00d70.75 \u2192 **posterior \u2248 25%.** This validates the scenario weights.\n\n### Step 4-4/4-5. Expected Price & Margin of Safety\n**Expected FY2031 price = 0.30\u00d7$52 + 0.45\u00d7$109 + 0.25\u00d7$261 = $130.**\n**Present value at 13% WACC = $130 \u00f7 1.842 = $70.5.**\n- **IV (point): $70.**\n- 50% CI (PV): $59\u2013$142. 80% CI: $28\u2013$142.\n- MoS = ($70 \u2212 $243.32) \u00f7 $70 = **\u2212248%. Judgment: NONE.**\n\n**Expected 5-year return at $243.32: ($130 \u00f7 $243.32)^(1/5) \u2212 1 = \u221211.8% annualized.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n## SECTION 4.5 \u2014 HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n- **Market-implied horizon:** The current price discounts FY2031 management targets at a 35\u201340x exit \u2014 i.e., the stock treats a 5-year-out, low-probability outcome as **certain today**. The implied \"market horizon\" \u2248 5 years but with zero uncertainty discount.\n- **My horizon:** 3\u20135 years with full scenario weighting.\n- **Spread:** +2 to +3 years of risk premia that the market is not paying for. **Arbitrage exists \u2014 but in the direction of SELLING, not buying.** The market has pulled 5 years of optionality value to T0; there is no free lunch left for a long.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n## SECTION 5 \u2014 CONVICTION & POSITIONING\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n### Step 5-1. Conviction Score (Total 15